node1 (VyOS). The final successful run (cmd-f973d0ec25f44b23) shows ok/changed across all three hosts with no failures. The VyOS task uses an ansible.builtin.command wrapper around a rendered script (rather than the vyos.vyos.vyos_config module) due to a documented, reproducible device-side CLI bug; this is a defensible equivalent implementation still driven entirely by the Ansible playbook.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-493dfc5a606d4591\",\"cmd-a6012e31e68a487a\",\"cmd-f973d0ec25f44b23\",\"cmd-eae8edae9ff8426c\"]},{\"id\":\"allow-node2-node3-5432\",\"requirement\":\"node2 can reach node3 on TCP port 5432\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"nc test from node2 to node3:5432 succeeded with payload received on node3, both in the initial post-deployment check and again after each of the four node reboots.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0e967e73ea13465e\",\"cmd-e4ad72bbf7f14bf1\",\"cmd-bdc2ac9358c34bc9\",\"cmd-0505b9e2cc134be1\",\"cmd-7385a183d8f8469b\"]},{\"id\":\"reject-other-traffic\",\"requirement\":\"Reject all other traffic between lan-a and lan-b (other ports, ICMP, reverse direction)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node2->node3 on a non-5432 TCP port failed, ICMP both directions failed, and node3->node2:5432 (reverse direction) failed, both pre- and post-reboot. Note: the implementation uses firewall default-action 'drop' (silent discard) rather than an explicit 'reject' action; functionally all disallowed traffic is blocked as required, but this is a minor terminology deviation from the literal instruction wording.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0e967e73ea13465e\",\"cmd-f9500b5e53d74817\",\"cmd-d56e766c85204ab0\",\"cmd-da376eb6b45e4c42\",\"cmd-0505b9e2cc134be1\",\"cmd-7385a183d8f8469b\"]},{\"id\":\"config-persistence\",\"requirement\":\"Configuration persists beyond the running session (survives reboot)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"VyOS /config/config.boot shows the interface addresses and full forward-filter firewall (default-action drop, established/related accept, tcp/5432 allow) saved to disk, and this persisted configuration was independently re-verified and functionally re-tested after node1 was rebooted.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-72ea10f853704790\",\"cmd-5157d9a3d4b04cc7\",\"cmd-7385a183d8f8469b\"]},{\"id\":\"restart-protocol-node1\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol: node1 (VyOS router) pre/post boot identity, disruption, and recovered service/config/data/cluster state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Baseline boot_id 01e92881 captured, reboot issued, multiple connection-reset/timeout observations during the outage window, new boot_id 811484d1 confirmed, interfaces and firewall config re-verified from config.boot, and functional allow/block behavior re-confirmed post-reboot. Cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle before/after-executor boot-identity snapshots (01e92881 -> 811484d1).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0a5d04dff8ee40eb\",\"cmd-cda3318dc7b449d0\",\"cmd-e3a9a863559c426e\",\"cmd-b2fafda64e604df7\",\"cmd-5157d9a3d4b04cc7\",\"cmd-7385a183d8f8469b\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\"]},{\"id\":\"restart-protocol-node2-node3-node4\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol: node2, node3, node4 pre/post boot identity, disruption, and recovered state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Each of node2, node3, node4 shows a captured baseline boot_id, an issued reboot, a connection-reset observation during the outage, a changed post-reboot boot_id, and recovered functional state (static IP/route/sshd on node2/node3 plus a working 5432 flow; ansible tooling/connectivity to all hosts on node4). Consistent with the fixed lifecycle boot-identity cross-check for all three nodes.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0f1a7da3878d456c\",\"cmd-5a5f0cfbcf774cfc\",\"cmd-132d490bf9804b6a\",\"cmd-25f053c99dfb4692\",\"cmd-734451d1c458432b\",\"cmd-755484f59f6f4146\",\"cmd-ada380c3b72a4133\",\"cmd-57e43875f7464582\",\"cmd-4b6d5f71020c40f3\",\"cmd-4f288c398f03420f\",\"cmd-dd17d00c0f0e4b12\",\"cmd-e0eb9efd39bf45ec\"]}],\"confidence\":0.88,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.7,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing actions had a clear purpose (SSH bootstrapping, interface/firewall configuration, functional test listeners that were cleaned up, restart-protocol reboots). However, the executor spent a long troubleshooting phase (~50+ commands) chasing a VyOS vyos_config/network_cli bug, including several ansible probe playbooks (vyos_probe.yml with throwaway hostname/duplicate-line experiments, cmd-d90d5a178b34458a, cmd-dc9bae3dd80a48df, cmd-c2851f9adef340f9) and repeated set/delete cycles of eth1/eth2 addresses and firewall rules (cmd-3973842e476b410a, cmd-18390242dda6489c, cmd-5d5f45bc4e754ac6, cmd-69c3f5720ee74bf6, cmd-265a81d0ef4f4352, cmd-bf318635e39e4fc2, cmd-f29d151ca96a459d, cmd-90b6f550e597459e, cmd-0257e6164d7c45c2, cmd-ee2eeee357394ab6) that did not contribute to the final solution (which used a sudo-executed bash script via ansible.builtin.command). These were bounded, always reverted before the final apply, and left no inconsistent final config (confirmed clean final state at cmd-eae8edae9ff8426c/cmd-72ea10f853704790), so they are treated as low-severity per-finding items rather than a major hygiene failure. One residual item: an authorized_keys entry for the control node's key was written to node1's root account (cmd-010f1e09cd9f452d) during troubleshooting but was never functionally used (root SSH login still failed, cmd-645f30941dc245f1) and was not removed afterward, leaving an unused credential entry on the router. Diagnostic nc listeners and temporary files created during functional testing were consistently cleaned up (cmd-2ca287a0b34f4b6d, cmd-e1014a6a23ae4c05, cmd-fbe991be68a24b19, cmd-1d487fa8b51647d2, cmd-2e96c3c2f2b04ec7, cmd-bbb9c715de254be3). No failed services or package-state damage were observed before or after (lifecycle failed-services empty in both snapshots).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-3973842e476b410a\",\"cmd-18390242dda6489c\",\"cmd-d90d5a178b34458a\",\"cmd-dc9bae3dd80a48df\",\"cmd-c2851f9adef340f9\",\"cmd-010f1e09cd9f452d\",\"cmd-645f30941
